00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:20.843
Yeah, so I grew up in Washington, DC.

776
00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:26.000
I was a swimmer, went to Stanford, swam there, graduated,

777
00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:28.130
lived in New York City for a couple of years,

778
00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:31.590
went upstate to law school for a couple of years

779
00:35:31.590 --> 00:35:33.650
but spent most of my time in Manhattan

780
00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:36.530
getting into trouble and screwing up my life.

781
00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.240
Moved to San Francisco, was a lawyer there

782
00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:39.073
for a couple of years.

783
00:35:39.073 --> 00:35:43.940
Moved here in '96, was a lawyer for a couple of years,

784
00:35:44.840 --> 00:35:47.330
bottomed out on drugs and alcohol.

785
00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:50.770
Went to treatment got sober, sobriety was my job.

786
00:35:50.770 --> 00:35:53.500
I still worked as a lawyer for many years after that

787
00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:55.470
but sobriety was the thing, you know,

788
00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:58.570
and that was my introduction to tools and new principles

789
00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:01.420
for how to organize my life and live.

790
00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:04.370
And that's still the lens through which I, you know,

791
00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.910
process everything that I do.

792
00:36:06.910 --> 00:36:10.900
But that was, I mean, I got sober in '98

793
00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:14.970
and I feel like only now I'm starting to really feel

794
00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:19.320
like I'm expressing the authentic version of who I am.

795
00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:20.153
Whoa.

796
00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:25.683
Yeah, I mean, the brutality of sobriety,

797
00:36:27.941 --> 00:36:28.774
(speakers talking over each other)

798
00:36:28.774 --> 00:36:30.101
It is unbelievable how hard it is.

799
00:36:30.101 --> 00:36:31.290
Are you comfortable talking about that?

800
00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:34.750
I am, I called a friend before I did my sobriety movie

801
00:36:34.750 --> 00:36:36.770
because there's the traditions.

802
00:36:36.770 --> 00:36:38.990
One of the things is there's this quotation

803
00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:41.370
about in something, something, something

804
00:36:41.370 --> 00:36:43.500
in television and radio.

805
00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:46.110
And it's like in one of the traditions.

806
00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:49.230
We were anonymous at the level of press radio and film.

807
00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:50.470
So it's always a tricky thing.

808
00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:53.050
And as a sort of public facing person myself,

809
00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:54.660
I struggle with the tension

810
00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:58.060
between wanting to speak about these issues,

811
00:36:58.060 --> 00:37:03.060
'cause again, it's sort of like that campus job center,

812
00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:07.690
like there wasn't a lot of information.

813
00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:10.040
And I think that the downside of that tradition

814
00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:12.620
is that there's a lot of people who don't really understand

815
00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:14.810
what's available to them when they're in pain

816
00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:16.030
and struggling.

817
00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:20.810
So I try to walk that tight rope and share my...

818
00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:23.840
It's always experience-based, I just share my experience.

819
00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:26.210
I try to steer clear of being specific

820
00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:30.280
about the 12 step program itself.

821
00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:33.950
But everybody has different opinions

822
00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:35.640
about where that line lives.

823
00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:40.640
Yeah, I mean, lucky for me, like, if you like stories,

824
00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:43.660
man, that is your place that you go

825
00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:47.660
and you will hear the most incredible things,

826
00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:49.110
the most incredible stories.

827
00:37:49.110 --> 00:37:51.950
And then when you hear someone tell your story,

828
00:37:51.950 --> 00:37:53.750
that's like, that's the biggie.

829
00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:55.960
That's the one where you're like, okay,

