And what I didn't realize at the time

1771
01:21:48.020 --> 01:21:51.840
was how much work Casey was doing

1772
01:21:51.840 --> 01:21:53.700
on the business side of things,

1773
01:21:53.700 --> 01:21:55.680
how many meetings he was taking,

1774
01:21:55.680 --> 01:21:57.940
how many things he was writing up,

1775
01:21:57.940 --> 01:22:00.110
all the numbers and things and all this stuff.

1776
01:22:00.110 --> 01:22:03.040
I just thought that we were these two super talented

1777
01:22:03.040 --> 01:22:06.290
Bohemian dudes and everything was just working out for us

1778
01:22:06.290 --> 01:22:08.060
'cause I wasn't doing any of that stuff.

1779
01:22:08.060 --> 01:22:12.510
And so he put together some work for us to do

1780
01:22:12.510 --> 01:22:17.220
like some jobs that he and Tom went out and got.

1781
01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:18.767
And I was just like, "No, I'm not doing that.

1782
01:22:18.767 --> 01:22:21.820
"Let's fucking keep doing what we did with the thing."

1783
01:22:21.820 --> 01:22:25.440
And it was just like, you know, we got to keep...

1784
01:22:25.440 --> 01:22:27.830
This studio costs a lot of money to support

1785
01:22:27.830 --> 01:22:28.880
and blah, blah, blah.

1786
01:22:28.880 --> 01:22:31.177
And so I was just like, "Well, okay, fine.

1787
01:22:31.177 --> 01:22:33.147
"Then I won't be a part of the studio anymore.

1788
01:22:33.147 --> 01:22:36.520
"I'll just pack up and I'll move out to Los Angeles"

1789
01:22:36.520 --> 01:22:37.730
because that was what I wanted to do.

1790
01:22:37.730 --> 01:22:39.210
New York was really, like I said before,

1791
01:22:39.210 --> 01:22:41.500
it was just like really grinding on me

1792
01:22:41.500 --> 01:22:45.240
and that's what I did.

1793
01:22:45.240 --> 01:22:48.833
And then when we didn't get a second season of the TV show,

1794
01:22:50.420 --> 01:22:51.737
it was just like, "Okay, keep going."

1795
01:22:51.737 --> 01:22:53.770
And I had that arrogance to just keep going

1796
01:22:53.770 --> 01:22:54.900
and keep going and keep going.

1797
01:22:54.900 --> 01:22:56.790
And I've heard this story so many times.

1798
01:22:56.790 --> 01:23:00.431
There's this guy, I can't remember his last name.

1799
01:23:00.431 --> 01:23:01.550
It seems like, I think Wayne White,

1800
01:23:01.550 --> 01:23:03.890
the guy who he does the paintings.

1801
01:23:03.890 --> 01:23:08.640
Oh, the cartoonist who's a musician?

1802
01:23:08.640 --> 01:23:09.870
Pee-wee's Playhouse?

1803
01:23:09.870 --> 01:23:11.900
Yes, they made the documentary about him.

1804
01:23:11.900 --> 01:23:15.600
Yes, called "Beauty is Embarrassing."

1805
01:23:15.600 --> 01:23:17.360
And his story is very similar to mine

1806
01:23:17.360 --> 01:23:20.360
and it's just like grinding yourself to the bone

1807
01:23:20.360 --> 01:23:22.540
until it's just like, "No, this isn't right."

1808
01:23:22.540 --> 01:23:27.180
So I came out to LA and I thought the whole thing

1809
01:23:27.180 --> 01:23:28.893
was like, make feature films.

1810
01:23:29.870 --> 01:23:32.200
Learn this whole thing of making these.

1811
01:23:32.200 --> 01:23:35.180
I didn't realize we were pioneering anything.

1812
01:23:35.180 --> 01:23:38.450
I thought we were just in the minor leagues

1813
01:23:38.450 --> 01:23:42.190
trying to get the bigs, the guide the...

1814
01:23:42.190 --> 01:23:45.680
What's the guy from the Brooklyn Dodgers who signed kids

1815
01:23:45.680 --> 01:23:48.200
the greatest name, the guy who signed Jackie Robinson?

1816
01:23:48.200 --> 01:23:51.910
Branch Rickey, Branch Ricky is gonna come down and sign us

1817
01:23:51.910 --> 01:23:53.697
and we're gonna get the thing.

1818
01:23:53.697 --> 01:23:55.910
But you can't be blamed for having that idea,

1819
01:23:55.910 --> 01:23:58.017
I mean, that was the idea of the time.

1820
01:23:58.017 --> 01:23:59.330
I mean, Casey went through that as well

1821
01:23:59.330 --> 01:24:02.630
with producing that Safdie brothers movie

1822
01:24:02.630 --> 01:24:03.710
and have that experience.

1823
01:24:03.710 --> 01:24:05.370
And I know those guys are part of your,

1824
01:24:05.370 --> 01:24:07.490
like sort of creative collective.

1825
01:24:07.490 --> 01:24:09.400
That was the path.

1826
01:24:09.400 --> 01:24:11.560
People forget when the Neistat brothers came out,

1827
01:24:11.560 --> 01:24:13.430
there was no YouTube.

1828
01:24:13.430 --> 01:24:14.857
And you were making viral videos

1829
01:24:14.857 --> 01:24:18.310
before there was even a means for something to go viral.

1830
01:24:18.310 --> 01:24:22.170
The pipes, there was no distribution channel at the time.

1831
01:24:22.170 --> 01:24:27.170
So how would one know that this was this impending future

1832
01:24:27.210 --> 01:24:28.050
that would create a career path.

1833
01:24:28.050 --> 01:24:30.757
You have to ask Casey Neistat 'cause he knew.

1834
01:24:30.757 --> 01:24:31.590
Yeah, only somebody like Casey.

1835
01:24:31.590 --> 01:24:32.870
And so the way I think about this

1836
01:24:32.870 --> 01:24:36.040
and tell me if this is fair or way off base,

1837
01:24:36.040 --> 01:24:40.350
like certainly both of you guys are our artists.

1838
01:24:40.350 --> 01:24:44.620
I think the differentiator is there's a little bit more

1839
01:24:44.620 --> 01:24:48.180
of purity in terms of how you think about art

1840
01:24:48.180 --> 01:24:49.080
and creativity.

1841
01:24:49.080 --> 01:24:54.080
Whereas Casey is this entrepreneur business person

1842
01:24:55.840 --> 01:24:59.900
who's figured out this perfect combination

1843
01:24:59.900 --> 01:25:04.660
of art and commerce at just the exact correct

1844
01:25:04.660 --> 01:25:08.240
cultural moment for him to ignite a wildfire.

1845
01:25:08.240 --> 01:25:11.860
When I, over many years of knowing Casey,

1846
01:25:11.860 --> 01:25:15.480
what gets overlooked in terms of how people think about him

1847
01:25:15.480 --> 01:25:17.870
is the fact that he's a fucking genius

1848
01:25:17.870 --> 01:25:19.470
when it comes to marketing and business.

1849
01:25:19.470 --> 01:25:21.500
Like he understands that side of things

1850
01:25:21.500 --> 01:25:23.870
in a way that very few people do.

1851
01:25:23.870 --> 01:25:28.740
And he's able to cast that sensibility with great foresight.

1852
01:25:28.740 --> 01:25:30.480
Like he knows what's coming,

1853
01:25:30.480 --> 01:25:34.147
he understands what's important and what's not.

1854
01:25:34.147 --> 01:25:39.147
And he melds that with an expertise in storytelling,

1855
01:25:39.330 --> 01:25:43.430
like his vlogs, it's not just that he made 800 movies

1856
01:25:43.430 --> 01:25:45.890
in a row, like they're all really good

1857
01:25:45.890 --> 01:25:47.500
and they all have three act structure

1858
01:25:47.500 --> 01:25:49.770
and they're extraordinarily well edited

1859
01:25:49.770 --> 01:25:53.060
and conceptually coherent in their own regard.

1860
01:25:53.060 --> 01:25:55.914
Like that's a feat that I don't know

1861
01:25:55.914 --> 01:25:58.530
that anybody else on earth could have accomplished.

1862
01:25:58.530 --> 01:26:02.830
And so I don't think that yes, people love his videos

1863
01:26:02.830 --> 01:26:04.880
but I don't think they understand why they love them

1864
01:26:04.880 --> 01:26:07.320
and why they're so good and what makes them work.

1865
01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:11.910
And it's that engine behind the scenes that fuels

1866
01:26:11.910 --> 01:26:14.712
that whole thing from that very specific sensibility

1867
01:26:14.712 --> 01:26:16.110
that he has.

1868
01:26:16.110 --> 01:26:19.977
But when I look at you, I see somebody who thought,

1869
01:26:19.977 --> 01:26:23.317
"You know, I'm an artist, I'm gonna continue

1870
01:26:23.317 --> 01:26:27.917
"to be this Bohemian guy and I'll figure it out as I go."

1871
01:26:32.299 --> 01:26:35.290
I think in the end, we do different emotions,

1872
01:26:37.800 --> 01:26:42.800
like Casey does exuberance and comedy, he's hilarious.

1873
01:26:43.440 --> 01:26:47.390
And I do, I don't know introspection,

1874
01:26:47.390 --> 01:26:51.253
and I don't know, insight or something, I don't know.

1875
01:26:53.040 --> 01:26:56.460
I think that's correct, yeah.

1876
01:26:56.460 --> 01:26:57.293
The combination--

1877
01:26:57.293 --> 01:27:00.660
Comedy and fun is way more fun than--

1878
01:27:00.660 --> 01:27:03.130
Well, that's gonna travel on the internet.

1879
01:27:03.130 --> 01:27:04.280
You know what I mean?

1880
01:27:05.490 --> 01:27:08.752
But as far as like, I mean what he's done

1881
01:27:08.752 --> 01:27:12.720
and trying to do this thing, like what he's done

1882
01:27:12.720 --> 01:27:16.480
is a really significant artistic feat.

1883
01:27:16.480 --> 01:27:21.480
And I know that we have friends that say that stuff too,

1884
01:27:21.960 --> 01:27:23.360
our close friends.

1885
01:27:23.360 --> 01:27:28.200
We have allies, old old friends, like from the neighborhood

1886
01:27:28.200 --> 01:27:30.570
and stuff that like this was my old friend

1887
01:27:30.570 --> 01:27:33.180
and he says what you just said to me, he says that to me.

1888
01:27:33.180 --> 01:27:34.690
And then Casey has his old friends

1889
01:27:34.690 --> 01:27:36.370
and they say whatever they say to him

1890
01:27:36.370 --> 01:27:38.493
and Casey has the success and the thing.

1891
01:27:41.360 --> 01:27:46.360
But the way I look at it, it doesn't feel like I'm an artist

1892
01:27:46.610 --> 01:27:47.830
and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

1893
01:27:47.830 --> 01:27:51.850
It feels like I can't do it any other way.

1894
01:27:51.850 --> 01:27:55.360
It's not that it's like, okay, fine, put Michael Jordan

1895
01:27:55.360 --> 01:27:58.910
behind the wheel of Lewis Hamilton's Mercedes.

1896
01:27:58.910 --> 01:28:00.460
He's not gonna do it.

1897
01:28:00.460 --> 01:28:02.670
I don't care how good Michael Jordan is.

1898
01:28:02.670 --> 01:28:04.720
He's not gonna get it around the track as fast

1899
01:28:04.720 --> 01:28:06.360
as Lewis Hamilton.

1900
01:28:06.360 --> 01:28:08.380
And that's really what it feels like.

1901
01:28:08.380 --> 01:28:11.250
And it's like, maybe because I worked with Sachs

1902
01:28:11.250 --> 01:28:16.250
and his instruction, he is an incredibly good teacher.

1903
01:28:19.099 --> 01:28:21.410
And one of the things he taught you,

1904
01:28:21.410 --> 01:28:24.150
he taught people who listened was the...

1905
01:28:29.293 --> 01:28:31.820
Okay, we're writing a studio manual, Sachs and I

1906
01:28:31.820 --> 01:28:34.047
for his studio and one of the chapters is called,

1907
01:28:34.047 --> 01:28:37.307
"Pretension, How to Avoid It and How to Exploit It."

1908
01:28:38.960 --> 01:28:39.793
So he went--

1909
01:28:39.793 --> 01:28:42.233
Sound like a human resources manual.

1910
01:28:43.080 --> 01:28:44.730
Part that, it's a lot that.

1911
01:28:44.730 --> 01:28:48.510
But there's this thing where...

1912
01:28:50.210 --> 01:28:53.050
There's a problem with art and with filmmaking

1913
01:28:53.050 --> 01:28:57.440
and with acting, and the problem with it is that it's cool

1914
01:28:57.440 --> 01:28:59.490
and it's perceived as cool.

1915
01:28:59.490 --> 01:29:04.490
And that's a problem because sometimes it's cool, sure.

1916
01:29:05.620 --> 01:29:08.973
But that's not what it is, you know what I mean?

1917
01:29:18.518 --> 01:29:21.937
It's a job, you don't go into it because it's cool.

1918
01:29:21.937 --> 01:29:25.330
And a lot of people do go into it because it's cool.

1919
01:29:25.330 --> 01:29:29.527
And when you say, "Oh, I do it because I'm an artist

1920
01:29:29.527 --> 01:29:30.970
"and I wanna do it this way."

1921
01:29:30.970 --> 01:29:35.350
Like that sounds cool, but that's not really,

1922
01:29:35.350 --> 01:29:37.150
that's not what it feels like.

1923
01:29:37.150 --> 01:29:39.683
I just feel like I didn't prioritize.

1924
01:29:40.560 --> 01:29:43.500
I'm always hoping people will say, will ask me

1925
01:29:44.560 --> 01:29:46.853
what's the worst advice you've ever gotten.

1926
01:29:47.780 --> 01:29:50.490
And to me, the worst advice I've ever gotten, I think

1927
01:29:50.490 --> 01:29:55.490
is do what you love and the money will come automatically.

1928
01:29:56.260 --> 01:29:57.850
That is fucking preposterous.

1929
01:29:57.850 --> 01:30:02.850
I wasted 15 years doing that and it's not the fuck true.

1930
01:30:02.910 --> 01:30:06.030
Like you really have to put...

1931
01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:07.400
Everyone needs money.

1932
01:30:07.400 --> 01:30:09.407
Everyone needs to have money unless you're born with it

1933
01:30:09.407 --> 01:30:11.420
and you still need it.

1934
01:30:11.420 --> 01:30:13.990
You really have to prioritize and understand

1935
01:30:13.990 --> 01:30:17.590
that making money is a skill and is a craft.

1936
01:30:17.590 --> 01:30:19.720
That's not automatic, it's very intentional

1937
01:30:19.720 --> 01:30:22.140
and you have to go after and do it.

1938
01:30:22.140 --> 01:30:24.710
Casey learned that at 16

1939
01:30:24.710 --> 01:30:25.830
when he got his girlfriend pregnant.

1940
01:30:25.830 --> 01:30:27.210
I was there, man, I was there--

1941
01:30:27.210 --> 01:30:29.113
When he left home and moved in with you, right?

1942
01:30:29.113 --> 01:30:30.483
Yes, I remember that whole thing coming--

1943
01:30:30.483 --> 01:30:32.210
That had to be insane.

1944
01:30:32.210 --> 01:30:33.473
What was going on with you when he--

1945
01:30:33.473 --> 01:30:38.473
I was just like, I mean yeah, I mean, it was like.

1946
01:30:39.663 --> 01:30:42.890
(speakers talking over each other)

1947
01:30:42.890 --> 01:30:45.910
And lived with you when you were in college, right?

1948
01:30:45.910 --> 01:30:48.263
Rich, he was so at ease with it.

1949
01:30:49.100 --> 01:30:51.020
He was just like, "Yeah."

1950
01:30:51.020 --> 01:30:53.810
He was like, "So yeah, I'm gonna have a baby."

1951
01:30:53.810 --> 01:30:56.777
I'm like, "You're 16 years old right now."

1952
01:30:59.130 --> 01:31:00.760
Like, "Are you kidding me?"

1953
01:31:00.760 --> 01:31:05.760
And then like, I can't, I mean, I can't believe.

1954
01:31:05.770 --> 01:31:08.570
Okay, so on top of all that, he's a dad.

1955
01:31:08.570 --> 01:31:11.770
On top of all that stuff he did, he's also a dad

1956
01:31:11.770 --> 01:31:14.270
and he's like a high school dropout.

1957
01:31:14.270 --> 01:31:17.830
And so I just think that, I mean I don't know,

1958
01:31:17.830 --> 01:31:20.610
it's not intentional, I wish I had made more money.

1959
01:31:20.610 --> 01:31:22.950
I wish I had, I would have been...

1960
01:31:22.950 --> 01:31:25.810
I think what he did is more pure than what I did

1961
01:31:25.810 --> 01:31:30.210
because he just unconsciously did it

1962
01:31:30.210 --> 01:31:34.970
and look at how much work he has, you know?

1963
01:31:34.970 --> 01:31:36.530
Sure, but then he had to take out

1964
01:31:36.530 --> 01:31:38.833
an entire year and a half off.

1965
01:31:39.780 --> 01:31:42.980
Oh, if I could do that, I'd never make another video.

1966
01:31:44.707 --> 01:31:45.640
That's interesting that you say that

1967
01:31:45.640 --> 01:31:48.881
because we just got off this jag of you talking

1968
01:31:48.881 --> 01:31:53.881
about the disconnect between the romantic ideal

1969
01:31:55.000 --> 01:31:57.190
of the artist, the person who thinks it's cool

1970
01:31:57.190 --> 01:31:58.023
to be an artist.

1971
01:31:58.023 --> 01:32:03.023
You look at Tom Sachs and you're wow, like huge, cool lofts

1972
01:32:03.390 --> 01:32:05.209
and like lots of cool stuff.

1973
01:32:05.209 --> 01:32:07.140
It's like, it's sexy.

1974
01:32:07.140 --> 01:32:10.510
But the artist is the person who creates

1975
01:32:12.780 --> 01:32:14.570
because there is no other choice.

1976
01:32:14.570 --> 01:32:16.621
There is something inside that person

1977
01:32:16.621 --> 01:32:18.700
that is yearning to be expressed.

1978
01:32:18.700 --> 01:32:23.700
It's a specific lens on an experience or an event

1979
01:32:24.270 --> 01:32:28.230
that in the expression says something more broadly

1980
01:32:28.230 --> 01:32:30.420
about humanity or the world that we live in.

1981
01:32:30.420 --> 01:32:34.300
And that person cannot rest until that exists in the world.

1982
01:32:34.300 --> 01:32:36.500
And so when I talk about purity,

1983
01:32:36.500 --> 01:32:39.660
like I sense that in yourself.

1984
01:32:39.660 --> 01:32:41.390
So it's interesting that you would say you would never make

1985
01:32:41.390 --> 01:32:43.260
another video again, like, I don't believe you,

1986
01:32:43.260 --> 01:32:44.560
I don't think that's true.

1987
01:32:45.750 --> 01:32:48.660
That's what it feels like, it feels like it.

1988
01:32:48.660 --> 01:32:52.110
I heard David Spade once say, "If I had my brother's money,"

1989
01:32:52.110 --> 01:32:55.520
his brother is Andy Spade, he's a friend of mine.

1990
01:32:55.520 --> 01:32:58.457
I said, "If I have my brother's money, I'd throw mine away."

1991
01:33:01.500 --> 01:33:02.333
That's funny.

1992
01:33:02.333 --> 01:33:03.330
I don't know, I just...

1993
01:33:03.330 --> 01:33:06.310
Oh wow, the work is really, it's very hard.

1994
01:33:06.310 --> 01:33:11.310
And I made a video or an episode called "Gratitude"

1995
01:33:12.920 --> 01:33:17.920
because it is so rare and so...

1996
01:33:18.010 --> 01:33:21.700
It is an incredible blessing to be able to make a living

1997
01:33:22.620 --> 01:33:25.020
out of the things that you make with your hands.

1998
01:33:28.720 --> 01:33:32.160
Another thing that came to me as a great relief

1999
01:33:32.160 --> 01:33:34.710
is I heard Jordan Peterson talking.

2000
01:33:34.710 --> 01:33:36.940
He's advising kids in college and he was talking

2001
01:33:36.940 --> 01:33:41.530
about how impossible it is to be a professional artist.

2002
01:33:41.530 --> 01:33:43.860
And he just goes one after another, after another,

2003
01:33:43.860 --> 01:33:46.910
after another and these are rational, rational,

2004
01:33:46.910 --> 01:33:48.200
correct reasons.

2005
01:33:48.200 --> 01:33:50.590
And hearing him say that and then knowing...

2006
01:33:54.350 --> 01:33:56.180
I've never really been able to pay all my bills

2007
01:33:56.180 --> 01:33:58.550
but I'm still doing it for 20 years.

2008
01:33:58.550 --> 01:34:00.540
I've been doing this thing

2009
01:34:00.540 --> 01:34:03.180
and I do have a tremendous amount of gratitude for it.

2010
01:34:03.180 --> 01:34:06.380
And at the same time, I'm wrestling with the resentment

2011
01:34:06.380 --> 01:34:08.580
of the reality of how hard it is.

2012
01:34:08.580 --> 01:34:13.257
And I just wish I would just lie and just say,

2013
01:34:13.257 --> 01:34:17.207
"Oh no, it's unbelievable, I wake up, I pop out of bed.

2014
01:34:17.207 --> 01:34:18.310
"It's unbelievable."

2015
01:34:18.310 --> 01:34:20.870
But really, and I hope that this is just a phase

2016
01:34:20.870 --> 01:34:23.590
and maybe when I get older it'll ease back.

2017
01:34:25.450 --> 01:34:28.320
But it it's as if I'm in a house that's on fire

2018
01:34:28.320 --> 01:34:31.410
and I have to just grab all the passports and the thing

2019
01:34:31.410 --> 01:34:34.480
and the kid and the star and try to put some of it out.

